{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import pathlib,random,sqlite3,sys\nc=sqlite3.connect((pathlib.Path(sys.argv[1])/'\\\\''linked-1-copy/views.sqlite'\\\\'').as_uri()+'\\\\''?mode=ro'\\\\'',uri=True)\nr=c.execute(\\\"select judgement_id,publication_day,implied_outcome,reasons from judgements where view_id='\\\\''v14'\\\\'' and publication_day>='\\\\''2026-07-10'\\\\'' order by judgement_id\\\").fetchall()\nrandom.Random(37).shuffle(r)\nfor i,(jid,day,forecast,reasons) in enumerate(r,1):\n if int(sys.argv[2])<=i<=int(sys.argv[3]):print(f\\\"{i:02d} | {forecast}\\\\n     | {reasons}\\\")\n if len(sys.argv)>4:print(i,jid,day)\n' '/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad' 1 18\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import pathlib,random,sqlite3,sys\nc=sqlite3.connect((pathlib.Path(sys.argv[1])/'\\\\''linked-1-copy/views.sqlite'\\\\'').as_uri()+'\\\\''?mode=ro'\\\\'',uri=True)\nr=c.execute(\\\"select judgement_id,publication_day,implied_outcome,reasons from judgements where view_id='\\\\''v14'\\\\'' and publication_day>='\\\\''2026-07-10'\\\\'' order by judgement_id\\\").fetchall()\nrandom.Random(37).shuffle(r)\nfor i,(jid,day,forecast,reasons) in enumerate(r,1):\n if int(sys.argv[2])<=i<=int(sys.argv[3]):print(f\\\"{i:02d} | {forecast}\\\\n     | {reasons}\\\")\n if len(sys.argv)>4:print(i,jid,day)\n' '/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad' 1 18\"","aggregated_output":"01 | Approximately $386 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1, plus $91 billion guided for Q2, then about $101 billion and $112 billion in Q3 and Q4 if Q2’s roughly 11% sequential growth carries forward.\n     | The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, which is rung 3. Carrying the Q2 guidance of $91 billion forward at its guided roughly 11% sequential growth rate implies about $101 billion in Q3 and $112 billion in Q4; with reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion, that gives approximately $386 billion for the year. Strong demand indicators, price increases, and improved supply access add upside, but the cited deals and product-sales targets are not sized as FY2027 NVIDIA revenue, while memory constraints and China restrictions are counterweights. The implied figure remains below $400 billion, so it does not cross into rung 2; it is also well above rung 4’s $350 billion boundary. The approximately $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU-sales forecast in v8 is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add to the total, and is consistent with this forecast.\n02 | Approximately $415 billion: Q1 actual revenue of $81.6 billion plus the $91 billion Q2 guide, with the guided 11% sequential growth carried into Q3 and Q4, implies about $386 billion before an uplift for demand and production evidence, tempered by supply and China risks.\n     | The reference is Bloomberg/Wall Street consensus of about $393 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 3. Carrying NVIDIA’s Q2 guide of $91 billion and its 11% sequential growth forward gives approximately $386 billion for the four quarters, below the $400 billion boundary; the forecast remains about $415 billion after a measured uplift for strong demand and production evidence, partly offset by memory and China-related constraints. That crosses into rung 2 but remains below $450 billion. The announced $1 trillion of Blackwell and Vera Rubin orders spans products and periods, so it supports demand but cannot be counted as FY2027 revenue in full. The approximately $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU forecast is a component of total revenue and is already contained within this estimate, not added to it.\n03 | About $393 billion for FY2027: Bloomberg’s quantified projection is the available revenue forecast, and the new observation that AI laboratories account for roughly 20% of demand has no period or revenue figure with which to adjust it. The dependency’s approximately $20 billion stand-alone CPU forecast is included within total revenue, not added to it.\n     | The reference is Bloomberg’s $393 billion FY2027 sales projection, which sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). The new demand-mix observation is directionally bullish but gives no period or revenue amount, so it does not support a quantified increase across the $400 billion boundary. NVIDIA’s approximately $20 billion stand-alone CPU forecast is part of total revenue and is not added to Bloomberg’s total; the implied total remains about $393 billion, consistent with that dependency.\n04 | About $385.7 billion: use reported FY2027 Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, then carry the guided 11% sequential growth forward to approximately $101.0 billion in Q3 and $112.1 billion in Q4.\n     | The reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 3; the reported 82% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion is consistent with that figure. The current-quarter $91 billion guidance, described as up 11% sequentially, implies roughly $81.615B + $91.0B + $101.0B + $112.1B = $385.7 billion if that sequential growth carries forward. New demand and production evidence leans positive, but the $1 trillion processor-sales potential spans calendar 2026 and 2027 and has no FY2027 revenue breakdown; the announced deployments and supply arrangements likewise do not quantify FY2027 revenue. The implied figure remains below the $400 billion rung-2 boundary. The v8 CPU forecast of about $17 billion is included within total revenue, not added to it.\n05 | About $404–405 billion: add reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion, an estimated $94–95 billion for Q2, about $107–108 billion for Q3, and roughly $120–121 billion for Q4 by carrying approximately 12% sequential growth forward.\n     | The latest stated FY2027 market estimate is $396 billion, which is rung 3. The quarter-by-quarter forecast is modestly higher: Q1 reported $81.6 billion, Q2 is plausibly about $94–95 billion despite the $91 billion guide, Q3 about $107–108 billion, and carrying roughly 12% sequential growth into Q4 gives about $120–121 billion, or roughly $404–405 billion for FY2027. That crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2, but not the $450 billion boundary. The risk of memory constraints and competing custom chips tempers the upside; the $1 trillion order-book claims span products and periods and are not counted as FY2027 revenue. NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 CPU-sales forecast in v8 is part of total revenue, not incremental to it.\n06 | Approximately $407.1 billion: $81.6B reported in Q1 plus $96.2B reported in Q2 plus the $108B Q3 midpoint guide, with the roughly 12.3% Q3 sequential growth carried forward to an estimated $121.3B in Q4.\n     | The reference is the latest supplied analyst expectation of $411 billion, which sits in rung 2 ($400 billion to less than $450 billion). The reported first two quarters and Q3 midpoint guidance imply $81.6B + $96.2B + $108B; carrying the Q3 sequential growth rate of about 12.3% forward for Q4 gives about $121.3B, or approximately $407.1B for FY2027. That remains in rung 2: it is about $7B above the $400B floor and about $43B short of rung 1. Strong demand, Rubin production, capacity investment, pricing plans and contracted/project demand support the run-rate, while memory, wafer, packaging, power and data-center constraints temper the upside. The announced AWS deployment and the $1T Blackwell/Rubin target span multiple periods, and the Australia figure describes infrastructure plans rather than NVIDIA revenue; they do not justify adding their full amounts to FY2027 revenue. No quantified revision in the supplied evidence moves the implied result across a boundary. NVIDIA's approximately $20B stand-alone CPU outlook in dependency v8 is part of total revenue, not an addition to it.\n07 | Applying the Q1-to-Q2 sequential growth rate (about 11.5%) to Q2's $91 billion guidance for Q3 and Q4 gives approximately $387 billion for FY2027. Adding the dependency's approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU estimate gives an implied total of about $406 billion, in rung 2.\n     | The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 projection, which is in rung 3. Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion imply about $387 billion for FY2027 if the Q1-to-Q2 sequential growth rate carries through Q3 and Q4; adding the dependency's approximately $19 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU estimate gives about $406 billion, above rung 2's $400 billion boundary. The $119 billion in supply-related commitments and management's order comments support demand but are not added directly to revenue. New evidence of HBM supply pressure and China-related restrictions is a downside risk, while the UAE export change is a partial offset; none supplies a quantified FY2027 revenue adjustment large enough to change the estimate across a rung boundary.\n08 | Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion, with the guided 11% sequential growth carried through Q3 and Q4, implies about $386 billion. Customer spending and order/supply signals support a modest uplift, while memory and China headwinds temper it, giving an implied FY2027 total of roughly $390–395 billion.\n     | The market reference is approximately $393 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion). The operating run-rate from Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, carrying the guided 11% sequential growth through Q3 and Q4, is about $386 billion. Recent customer spending and order visibility support a modest uplift from that run-rate, but the large Blackwell/Rubin figures span products and periods and are not fully attributable to FY2027; memory constraints and China exposure offset the upside. The implied outcome is around $390–395 billion, still below the $400 billion rung boundary. NVIDIA’s roughly $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU outlook is part of total revenue, not an addition to it.\n09 | About $410 billion: Q1 actual revenue of $81.6 billion, Q2 guidance of $91 billion and the cited Q3 estimate of $103.1 billion, with roughly 11% sequential growth carried into Q4, imply approximately $390 billion before an uplift for strong demand, booked commitments and pricing. Memory and China-related constraints temper that uplift.\n     | The reference is Bloomberg/Wall Street consensus of about $393 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 3. The operating run-rate is close to that reference: Q1 revenue was $81.6 billion, Q2 guidance is $91 billion, and the cited Q3 analyst estimate is about $103.1 billion; carrying roughly 11% sequential growth into Q4 implies about $114 billion and approximately $390 billion for the year. Strong customer demand, booked supply commitments, rising AI infrastructure spending and pricing support a modest uplift to about $410 billion, while memory constraints and China-related restrictions cap it. That forecast crosses $400 billion but remains below $450 billion. The roughly $1 trillion of chip orders spans products and periods and is not counted as FY2027 revenue in full. The dependency's approximately $20 billion stand-alone CPU forecast is a component of, and fits within, this total.\n10 | About $385.7 billion: $81.615 billion reported in Q1 plus $91 billion guided for Q2, followed by Q3 and Q4 at 11% sequential growth ($101.0 billion and $112.1 billion). This is below rung 3’s $400 billion upper boundary and above its $350 billion lower boundary.\n     | The reference is Bloomberg’s $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 3; the 80% growth consensus in e751 applied to FY2026 revenue also implies about $388.6 billion, in rung 3. The reported Q1 figure and Q2 guidance, carried forward at the guided 11% sequential growth rate, imply about $385.7 billion—still between the $350 billion and $400 billion rung boundaries. Demand commitments and the multi-year product-sales outlook support the upside, but they do not establish FY2027 total revenue at $400 billion; China restrictions and the specific HBM supply constraint are counterweights. The v8 CPU forecast is a component of total revenue and is consistent with, not additive to, this estimate.\n11 | Q1 and Q2 reported revenue sum to $177.8 billion; adding Q3 guidance of $108 billion and carrying its approximately 12% sequential growth forward implies Q4 revenue of about $121.3 billion. The resulting FY2027 total is approximately $407.1 billion.\n     | The market reference is LSEG's $396 billion FY2027 consensus, which sits in rung 3. The reported first two quarters total $177.8 billion; adding Q3 guidance of $108 billion and carrying its roughly 12% sequential growth forward gives about $121.3 billion for Q4, or approximately $407.1 billion for FY2027. That clears the $400 billion boundary but remains below $450 billion, so the forecast is rung 2. Supply constraints are a downside risk, but the evidence does not quantify a reduction large enough to move the implied total below $400 billion. The v8 estimate of about $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales is part of total revenue, not additive to it, and is consistent with this total.\n12 | Approximately $387.2 billion: Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.615B followed by Q2 guidance of $91.0B implies about 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that rate into Q3 and Q4 gives roughly $101.5B and $113.1B, respectively.\n     | The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 3; the separate 80% growth estimate implies about $389 billion from FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, also rung 3. Independently, carrying the Q1-to-Q2 guided sequential growth rate forward gives approximately $81.6B + $91.0B + $101.5B + $113.1B = $387.2B for FY2027. That is about $12.8B below rung 2's $400B boundary and above rung 3's $350B floor. Mass production and the stated multi-year GPU and processor opportunities support upside, but do not allocate enough volume or revenue specifically to FY2027 to take the forecast across $400B; China exclusions and memory supply concerns weigh the other way. The v8 forecast of roughly $17B in stand-alone CPU sales is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add on top of the total run-rate. The themes provide demand or policy tailwinds, but do not quantify an FY2027 revenue revision sufficient to change the rung.\n13 | About $407.1 billion: $81.6 billion Q1 actual + $96.2 billion Q2 actual + $108 billion Q3 guidance + about $121.3 billion for Q4, carrying forward the 12.3% sequential growth implied by the Q3 guide (108 × 1.123).\n     | The market reference is LSEG's $396 billion FY2027 consensus, which sits in rung 3; Bloomberg's $393 billion estimate is also in rung 3. The evidence-based estimate is about $407.1 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion, Q3 guidance of $108 billion, and Q4 of about $121.3 billion if the 12.3% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance carries forward. That crosses the $400 billion boundary but remains below $450 billion, so the forecast is rung 2. Newly detailed supply limits temper the outlook, but provide no quantified FY2027 reduction that would displace this run-rate estimate. The approximately $20 billion stand-alone CPU forecast is consistent with, and does not exceed, the total.\n14 | The reported $81.6 billion Q1 and $91 billion Q2 guide, carried forward at roughly 11% sequential growth, imply about $386 billion for FY2027 (Q3 about $101 billion; Q4 about $112 billion). Order visibility and pricing support upside toward roughly $393 billion, but the evidence does not imply crossing $400 billion.\n     | The reference is Bloomberg/Wall Street FY2027 revenue of about $393 billion, which sits in rung 3. Carrying the $91 billion Q2 guide forward at its roughly 11% sequential growth rate gives about $101 billion in Q3 and $112 billion in Q4; with reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion, that implies about $386 billion for FY2027. The roughly $1 trillion of confirmed Blackwell and Vera Rubin orders through 2027, supply commitments, and reported price increases support some upside toward the reference, but those figures span products and periods and do not establish at least $400 billion of FY2027 revenue. China remains a counterweight because Q1 shipments and Q2 guidance exclude China Data Center compute revenue. The approximately $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU forecast in v8 is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add on top of a total that already includes it; it is consistent with, and does not exceed, the total-revenue forecast.\n15 | About $385.7 billion: $81.615 billion reported for Q1 plus $91 billion guided for Q2, with the guided 11% sequential growth carried forward to approximately $101.0 billion in Q3 and $112.1 billion in Q4.\n     | The reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 3; the separate 80%–82% growth estimates also imply roughly $388–393 billion from FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion. The operating run-rate gives a lower point estimate: $81.615 billion in Q1 plus $91 billion guided for Q2, then carrying the guided 11% sequential growth forward for Q3 and Q4 implies about $385.7 billion for FY2027. Strong orders and announced demand support the upside, while the $1 trillion Blackwell/Vera Rubin figure spans multiple products and calendar years, and the memory and China evidence adds execution risk; neither supplies a quantified FY2027 uplift sufficient to take the forecast to $400 billion. The v8 estimate of $18–19 billion in stand-alone CPU sales is part of total revenue, not an addition to it.\n16 | Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion, Q3 guidance of $108 billion, and Q4 estimated by carrying Q3’s roughly 12% sequential growth forward ($121 billion) imply approximately $407 billion for FY2027.\n     | The market reference is analysts’ $411 billion FY2027 estimate, in rung 2; the newer Zacks estimate of 87.4% growth applied to FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion implies roughly $405 billion, also in rung 2. Independent of those consensus figures, Q1 and Q2 reported revenue plus Q3 guidance and a Q4 carry-forward imply about $407 billion. That is above rung 2’s $400 billion floor but below rung 1’s $450 billion threshold. Strong demand and orders provide upside potential, but the evidence does not quantify enough FY2027 revenue to cross $450 billion, while reported supply and data-center bottlenecks temper the forecast. The approximately $20 billion stand-alone CPU outlook in v8 is included in total revenue and is not added again.\n17 | About $410 billion: Q1 actual revenue ($81.615 billion) plus Q2 guidance ($91 billion), Q3 consensus ($103.1 billion), and an estimated roughly $114 billion in Q4 from carrying approximately 11% sequential growth forward gives about $390 billion; demand, orders and pricing support a modest uplift above that run-rate.\n     | The market reference is Bloomberg/Wall Street consensus of about $393 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 3. The run-rate implied by $81.615 billion in Q1, $91 billion Q2 guidance, the $103.1 billion Q3 analyst estimate, and roughly 11% sequential growth into Q4 is about $390 billion. Strong AI infrastructure demand, booked orders, deployments and pricing support a modest uplift to around $410 billion, crossing $400 billion into rung 2. Memory constraints and China-related restrictions temper the upside; the evidence does not imply $450 billion or more. The roughly $1 trillion in Blackwell and Vera Rubin orders spans products and periods, so it is not counted in full as FY2027 revenue. NVIDIA's stand-alone CPU sales forecast of about $20 billion is one component of total revenue, not an addition to the total forecast.\n18 | About $407 billion: $81.6 billion in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2 plus the $108 billion Q3 guidance midpoint, then approximately $121.3 billion for Q4 by carrying forward Q3’s 12.3% sequential growth.\n     | The market reference is LSEG’s $396 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits on rung 3. The reported Q1 and Q2 totals plus Q3 guidance, with Q4 modeled by carrying forward Q3’s sequential growth, imply about $407 billion. That crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2 but remains below $450 billion. The $1 trillion order-book figure and Rubin ramp support demand, but neither is a FY2027 recognized-revenue figure; memory, power and data-center constraints and custom-chip competition temper the upside. NVIDIA’s standalone CPU-sales estimate is part of total revenue, not incremental to it, and the dependent forecast remains consistent with a total above that component.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
