{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import pathlib,random,sqlite3,sys\nc=sqlite3.connect((pathlib.Path(sys.argv[1])/'\\\\''linked-1-copy/views.sqlite'\\\\'').as_uri()+'\\\\''?mode=ro'\\\\'',uri=True)\nr=c.execute(\\\"select judgement_id,publication_day,implied_outcome,reasons from judgements where view_id='\\\\''v14'\\\\'' and publication_day>='\\\\''2026-07-10'\\\\'' order by judgement_id\\\").fetchall()\nrandom.Random(37).shuffle(r)\nfor i,(jid,day,forecast,reasons) in enumerate(r,1):\n if int(sys.argv[2])<=i<=int(sys.argv[3]):print(f\\\"{i:02d} | {forecast}\\\\n     | {reasons}\\\")\n if len(sys.argv)>4:print(i,jid,day)\n' '/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad' 19 36\"","aggregated_output":"19 | Approximately $407.1 billion: reported Q1 and Q2 revenue of $81.6 billion and $96.2 billion, plus Q3 midpoint guidance of $108 billion, total $285.8 billion; carrying roughly 12.3% sequential growth forward gives Q4 of about $121.3 billion.\n     | The market reference is the latest cited Wall Street expectation of about $411 billion, which is rung 2; the earlier LSEG estimate of $396 billion was rung 3. The evidence-based run-rate remains just above the $400 billion boundary: Q1 and Q2 revenue total $177.8 billion, Q3 midpoint guidance is $108 billion, and carrying the roughly 12.3% sequential growth rate forward to Q4 implies about $121.3 billion, or approximately $407.1 billion for FY2027. Supply constraints temper that estimate, while the new 400,000-GPU deployment statement is not allocated to FY2027 or translated into NVIDIA revenue. Neither justifies moving below $400 billion or above $450 billion. The approximately $20 billion stand-alone CPU outlook in dependency v8 is included within, not added to, total revenue.\n20 | About $407 billion: $81.6 billion in Q1 plus $91 billion guided for Q2, with Q2's sequential growth carried into Q3 and Q4, implies roughly $387 billion; adding the dependency's approximately $19.5 billion stand-alone CPU forecast gives about $407 billion.\n     | The reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 projection, which sits in rung 3. The Q1 result and Q2 guidance imply about $387 billion for the year if Q2's sequential growth carries through Q3 and Q4; adding the dependency's approximately $19.5 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU forecast, treated as incremental to that run-rate, gives about $407 billion and crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2. Booked supply commitments and the updated AI-capex view support the upside, while memory-supply risk and custom-chip alternatives temper confidence; neither supplies a quantified adjustment that takes the implied estimate below $400 billion.\n21 | About $386 billion: carry the $91 billion Q2 guide forward at its roughly 11% sequential growth rate to estimate about $101 billion in Q3 and $112 billion in Q4, then add reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion. The demand evidence supports upside within the rung but does not quantify a sufficient FY2027 increment to imply $400 billion or more.\n     | The Bloomberg/Wall Street reference of $393 billion sits in rung 3. The $91 billion Q2 guide, up 11% sequentially, implies roughly $101 billion in Q3 and $112 billion in Q4 if that growth rate carries forward; with reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion, that gives about $386 billion for FY2027. Demand evidence supports the upper part of rung 3, but the roughly $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin order figure spans products and periods, and the deployment and customer-spending figures do not establish enough FY2027 NVIDIA revenue to cross $400 billion. China compute exclusions and memory-supply risks temper the upside. The v8 estimate of about $18 billion in stand-alone CPU revenue is consistent as a component of this total.\n22 | About $396 billion. Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion, Q2 guidance of $91 billion, the $103.1 billion Q3 estimate, and 12% sequential growth into Q4 imply roughly $391 billion; demand and orders support a modest uplift, but not enough to reach $400 billion.\n     | The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, which is rung 3; the 80% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $389 billion and is also rung 3. The operating path is similar: Q1 revenue was $81.6 billion, Q2 guidance is $91 billion, analysts put Q3 near $103.1 billion, and carrying roughly 12% sequential growth into Q4 gives about $115.5 billion, or roughly $391 billion for the year. Strong demand and confirmed orders support the upper end of the high-$390-billion range, but those orders span products and periods and do not establish enough FY2027 revenue to cross $400 billion; HBM constraints and China exposure are counterweights. The roughly $20 billion standalone-CPU outlook in v8 is consistent with a component of total revenue, not an amount to add on top of the total-revenue path.\n23 | The first two reported quarters total $177.8 billion. Add Q3's $108 billion midpoint guidance and carry its roughly 12.3% sequential growth into Q4 ($108 billion × 1.123 ≈ $121.3 billion), implying approximately $407.1 billion for FY2027.\n     | The reference is LSEG's $396 billion FY2027 consensus, which sits in rung 3. The reported first two quarters total $177.8 billion; adding Q3 guidance of $108 billion and carrying its roughly 12.3% sequential growth into Q4 implies about $121.3 billion for Q4 and approximately $407.1 billion for FY2027. That crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2, but remains below $450 billion. Capacity and memory constraints are downside risks, but the evidence does not quantify a shortfall sufficient to take the implied total below $400 billion. The v8 estimate of about $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales is included in total revenue, not additive. The new report that guidance was raised for the rest of the fiscal year is not quantified, so it does not change the calculated outcome or rung.\n24 | About $385.7 billion for FY2027: $81.615 billion reported in Q1 plus $91 billion guided for Q2, followed by Q3 and Q4 estimates of about $101.0 billion and $112.1 billion by carrying the 11% sequential growth in the Q2 outlook forward.\n     | The reference is Bloomberg’s $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 3; the reported 80%–82% growth estimates also imply roughly $389–$393 billion from FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion. The operating run-rate implies about $385.7 billion: $81.615 billion in Q1 plus $91 billion guided for Q2, then applying the guided 11% sequential growth to Q3 and Q4. Stronger AI spending, orders and supply partnerships tilt upward, but the $1 trillion product-sales forecast spans products and calendar years and supplies no FY2027 total-revenue allocation; HBM constraints and the Q2 exclusion of China compute sales are counterweights. The v8 CPU forecast of about $19 billion is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add again, and is consistent with this total. The implied figure remains below the $400 billion rung boundary.\n25 | About $419 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2’s $96.2 billion, an estimated Q3 of about $113.6 billion (the $110.16 billion high end of guidance adjusted by the reported 3.1% average beat), and Q4 of about $127.5 billion by carrying forward roughly 12% sequential growth.\n     | The market reference is Wall Street consensus of about $411 billion, which sits at rung 2; Zacks’ 87.4% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion also implies about $404.5 billion, at rung 2. The reported Q1 and Q2 figures plus Q3 guidance, adjusted for the quantified history of beating guidance and the $113–$114 billion Q3 estimate, imply about $419 billion for FY2027. Supply bottlenecks temper the upside, but this estimate remains above $400 billion and below the $450 billion boundary. The approximately $18 billion stand-alone CPU forecast in v8 is part of total revenue, not an amount to add, and is consistent with the total.\n26 | Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion followed by Q2 guidance of $91 billion implies 11.5% sequential growth. Carrying that growth into Q3 and Q4 yields approximately $387 billion for FY2027.\n     | The reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 3. Using Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion gives 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that growth through Q3 and Q4 implies about $387 billion for FY2027. That is below the reference but remains above rung 3's $350 billion floor and below rung 2's $400 billion boundary. Strong orders and AWS's stated 2027–2028 deployment plan support the outlook, but neither quantifies additional FY2027 revenue; China exclusions and memory-supply constraints weigh the other way. The current v8 forecast of $8–9 billion in stand-alone CPU sales is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add to the run-rate, and is consistent with total revenue remaining above it.\n27 | About $407 billion: $81.6B in Q1 plus $96.2B in Q2 plus Q3 guidance of $108B, with Q4 estimated at about $121.3B by carrying Q3’s implied 12.3% sequential growth forward.\n     | The market reference is about $411 billion, which sits in rung 2. The reported first two quarters and Q3 guidance imply $81.6B + $96.2B + $108B; carrying the 12.3% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance into Q4 gives about $121.3B, or roughly $407B for FY2027. That is below the consensus point but remains above the $400B rung boundary. Supply and memory constraints restrain the forecast, while strong demand, orders and updated AI-capex themes support it; none supplies a quantified FY2027 adjustment sufficient to move the estimate across a rung boundary. NVIDIA’s roughly $20B standalone-CPU forecast in v8 is part of total revenue, not an addition to it.\n28 | Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion followed by Q2 guidance of $91 billion implies about $387 billion for FY2027 if Q2's sequential growth rate carries through Q3 and Q4. Adding the dependency's approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU estimate as incremental implies about $406 billion.\n     | The reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 projection, which sits in rung 3. The Q1 result and Q2 guidance imply about $387 billion for the year if Q2's sequential growth carries through Q3 and Q4; adding the dependency's approximately $19 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU estimate as incremental gives about $406 billion, crossing the $400 billion boundary into rung 2. The $119 billion in supply-related commitments and Huang's comments about orders support demand, but do not translate directly into additional FY2027 revenue. Memory-supply constraints and China-related risks temper the outlook, but are not quantified enough to pull the estimate below $400 billion.\n29 | $81.6B Q1 actual + $96.2B Q2 actual + $108B Q3 guidance + about $121.3B for Q4 (carrying the roughly 12.3% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance forward from Q3) = approximately $407B for FY2027.\n     | The market reference is LSEG's $396 billion FY2027 consensus, with Bloomberg's $393 billion estimate also in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). The evidence-based estimate is about $407 billion: Q1 and Q2 actual revenue plus Q3 guidance, then Q4 estimated by carrying forward the 12.3% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance. That crosses the $400 billion rung boundary, but remains below $450 billion. Supply constraints temper the estimate, though the reported constraint is not quantified for the full year; the dependent stand-alone CPU forecast of about $20 billion is consistent with, and does not exceed, the total.\n30 | Carrying the Q2 guide of $91 billion, about 11.5% above Q1's $81.6 billion, forward for the remaining quarters gives approximately $101.5 billion in Q3 and $113.2 billion in Q4, or about $387 billion for FY2027. Robust booked demand supports a modest lift, but after discounting orders that span products and periods, the central estimate is roughly $390 billion.\n     | The Bloomberg/Wall Street FY2027 revenue reference of about $393 billion is in rung 3. The latest reported $81.6 billion Q1 and $91 billion Q2 guide, with the Q2 sequential growth rate carried through Q3 and Q4, imply about $387 billion; strong demand evidence supports a modest lift toward roughly $390 billion, but the $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin orders span products and periods and do not justify assigning that amount to FY2027. China restrictions and memory-supply risks temper the upside, so the forecast remains below the $400 billion rung boundary. The roughly $20 billion stand-alone CPU estimate in v8 is part of NVIDIA’s total, not an amount to add again.\n31 | Approximately $403.5 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1, about $94.5 billion for Q2 based on the $94–95 billion estimate, about $107.5 billion for Q3 per BofA, and about $119.9 billion for Q4 by carrying roughly 11.5% sequential growth forward. The $20 billion FY2027 CPU forecast in v8 is part of this total, not additional revenue.\n     | The FY2027 revenue reference is about $393 billion, which sits in rung 3. The forecast is modestly higher: Q1 was $81.6 billion; for Q2, the $91 billion company guide is the anchor, but upward revisions, the prior-quarter beat, and BofA’s $94–95 billion estimate support forecasting above the guide. Using roughly $94.5 billion for Q2, BofA’s $107–108 billion Q3 estimate, and carrying approximately 11.5% sequential growth into Q4 gives about $403.5 billion for the year, crossing the $400 billion boundary into rung 2. This is a narrow forecast crossing, not an established result; the $20 billion CPU outlook in v8 is included in total revenue, not added to it.\n32 | Roughly $404 billion on the base path: $81.6 billion Q1 actual, $94–95 billion Q2 estimate, $107–108 billion potential Q3 guidance, and approximately $120 billion for Q4 carrying forward about 12% sequential growth. The separate $96 billion Q2 / $112 billion Q3 estimate supports upside, but its implied Q4 is not treated as certain.\n     | The reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 consensus, which sits in rung 3. The stronger current-quarter forecast path implies roughly $404 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1, about $94–95 billion expected for Q2, $107–108 billion of potential Q3 guidance, then roughly $120 billion in Q4 if sequential growth continues near 12%. A newer, more bullish estimate of $96 billion for Q2 and $112 billion for Q3 supports further upside, while the $91 billion company Q2 guide and an estimate that 12% sequential growth would leave FY2027 just under $400 billion are counterweights. On balance, the implied result is modestly above $400 billion, crossing into rung 2; the $1 trillion order-book claim spans 2026 and 2027 and is not taken in full for FY2027.\n33 | Approximately $403.5 billion: $81.6 billion reported for Q1, BofA's $94.5 billion Q2 midpoint and $107.5 billion Q3 estimate, then about $119.9 billion for Q4 by carrying forward the approximately 11.5% sequential growth implied by Q2 guidance.\n     | The market reference is approximately $393 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 3. The newer forecast uses $81.6 billion reported in Q1, BofA's $94–95 billion Q2 estimate, its $107–108 billion estimate for potential Q3 guidance, and carries NVIDIA's roughly 11.5% sequential Q2 guided growth forward for Q4: approximately $81.6B + $94.5B + $107.5B + $119.9B = $403.5 billion. That narrowly crosses the $400 billion rung boundary. Q2 guidance itself remains $91 billion, and the Q3 figure is BofA's estimate rather than company guidance, so the crossing is a forecast, not an established result. The $20 billion FY2027 CPU forecast in v8 is included in—not added to—the total. Supply constraints remain a counterweight; the roughly $1 trillion of orders spans products and time and is not counted as FY2027 revenue.\n34 | About $393 billion in FY2027, using Bloomberg's specific sales projection (up 82%) as the forecast input. The dependency's approximately $20 billion CPU-sales figure is part of the total and does not raise it above the whole-revenue projection.\n     | The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 sales projection, which sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). The same quantified projection is the available forecast input; the bullish themes support demand directionally but do not quantify an adjustment that would carry the implied result across the $400 billion boundary. The dependency's approximately $20 billion CPU-sales forecast is a component of total revenue and is compatible with, not additive to, the $393 billion total.\n35 | About $419 billion: Q1 and Q2 actual revenue totals $177.8 billion; applying the 3.1% average beat to the high end of Q3 guidance implies about $113.6 billion for Q3, and carrying the guide’s 12% sequential growth into Q4 implies about $127.2 billion. This falls within rung 2’s $400–450 billion range.\n     | The current market reference is analysts’ $411 billion FY2027 estimate, in rung 2; Zacks’ 87.4% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion also implies roughly $405 billion, in rung 2. My estimate is about $419 billion: actual Q1 and Q2 revenue totaled $177.8 billion, Q3 is estimated at about $113.6 billion by applying the reported 3.1% average beat to the high end of guidance, and carrying the 12% sequential growth implied by the Q3 guide into Q4 gives about $127.2 billion. Supply and data-center bottlenecks temper the upside, but do not pull the estimate below $400 billion; it remains below rung 1’s $450 billion boundary. NVIDIA’s roughly $12 billion stand-alone CPU forecast is part of total revenue, not an addition to it, consistent with v8.\n36 | About $407.1 billion: $81.6 billion in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2 plus Q3 guidance of $108 billion, with Q4 estimated at about $121.3 billion by carrying forward Q3's 12.3% sequential growth.\n     | The market reference is LSEG's $396 billion FY2027 consensus, which is in rung 3. The evidence-based estimate is about $407.1 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion, Q3 guidance of $108 billion, and Q4 of about $121.3 billion if Q3's 12.3% sequential growth carries forward. That clears the $400 billion boundary but remains below $450 billion, so the forecast is rung 2. Supply constraints and the exclusion of China Data Center compute revenue temper the upside, but do not quantify a reduction sufficient to move the implied estimate below $400 billion. The approximately $20 billion stand-alone CPU outlook in dependency v8 is consistent with the total and does not exceed it.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import pathlib,random,sqlite3,sys\nc=sqlite3.connect((pathlib.Path(sys.argv[1])/'\\\\''linked-1-copy/views.sqlite'\\\\'').as_uri()+'\\\\''?mode=ro'\\\\'',uri=True)\nr=c.execute(\\\"select judgement_id,publication_day,implied_outcome,reasons from judgements where view_id='\\\\''v14'\\\\'' and publication_day>='\\\\''2026-07-10'\\\\'' order by judgement_id\\\").fetchall()\nrandom.Random(37).shuffle(r)\nfor i,(jid,day,forecast,reasons) in enumerate(r,1):\n if int(sys.argv[2])<=i<=int(sys.argv[3]):print(f\\\"{i:02d} | {forecast}\\\\n     | {reasons}\\\")\n if len(sys.argv)>4:print(i,jid,day)\n' '/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad' 37 54\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import pathlib,random,sqlite3,sys\nc=sqlite3.connect((pathlib.Path(sys.argv[1])/'\\\\''linked-1-copy/views.sqlite'\\\\'').as_uri()+'\\\\''?mode=ro'\\\\'',uri=True)\nr=c.execute(\\\"select judgement_id,publication_day,implied_outcome,reasons from judgements where view_id='\\\\''v14'\\\\'' and publication_day>='\\\\''2026-07-10'\\\\'' order by judgement_id\\\").fetchall()\nrandom.Random(37).shuffle(r)\nfor i,(jid,day,forecast,reasons) in enumerate(r,1):\n if int(sys.argv[2])<=i<=int(sys.argv[3]):print(f\\\"{i:02d} | {forecast}\\\\n     | {reasons}\\\")\n if len(sys.argv)>4:print(i,jid,day)\n' '/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad' 37 54\"","aggregated_output":"37 | The Q1 actual, Q2 guidance, Q3 estimate and continued sequential growth into Q4 imply a baseline around $390 billion. Allowing a modest uplift for demand and booked orders, while discounting multi-period orders and competitive, supply and China risks, implies roughly $400–410 billion.\n     | The market reference is Bloomberg/Wall Street FY2027 revenue consensus of about $393 billion, which sits in rung 3. Carrying the reported $81.6 billion Q1, $91 billion Q2 guidance, the $103.1 billion Q3 estimate and roughly 11% sequential growth into Q4 gives a baseline near $390 billion. Stronger demand and booked orders support a modest uplift: I forecast about $400–410 billion, crossing the $400 billion boundary into rung 2, but not the $450 billion boundary. The large order and infrastructure figures span products and periods, so they do not establish FY2027 revenue at their full amounts; competitive alternatives, memory constraints and China exposure also temper the upside. NVIDIA’s roughly $20 billion stand-alone CPU outlook is part of total revenue, not an additional amount to add to it.\n38 | About $419 billion: Q1 and Q2 total $177.8 billion; applying the historical average 3.1% beat above the $110.16 billion high end of Q3 guidance implies about $113.6 billion for Q3, and carrying the guided 12% sequential growth forward implies about $127.2 billion for Q4. The sum is approximately $418.6 billion.\n     | The market reference is about $411 billion of FY2027 revenue, which is rung 2; the 87.4% Zacks growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion implies about $404.5 billion, also rung 2. The evidence-derived estimate is about $419 billion, still rung 2 and below the $450 billion rung-1 boundary. Near-term supply constraints and the newly quantified unfulfilled-demand estimate temper the upside, but the latter concerns AI-infrastructure demand in 2027 and does not establish additional NVIDIA FY2027 revenue. The approximately $18 billion stand-alone CPU forecast in v8 is part of total revenue, not an amount to add to it, and is consistent with a total above that component.\n39 | About $400–410 billion, with a central estimate near $405 billion. Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion, Q2 guidance of $91 billion, the $103.1 billion Q3 estimate and an 11% sequential Q4 carry-forward imply a baseline near $390 billion; strong demand and booked supply support a modest uplift, not the full value of multi-period order figures.\n     | The market reference is FY2027 revenue consensus of about $393 billion, which is rung 3; the 80% growth consensus is also about $389 billion against FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion. The run-rate from reported Q1, Q2 guidance and the $103.1 billion Q3 estimate is about $390 billion if Q4 grows 11% sequentially from Q3. I forecast about $400–410 billion, around $405 billion: demand and booked-supply signals support a modest uplift over that run-rate, but the roughly $1 trillion order figure spans products and periods and is not FY2027 revenue guidance. NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU outlook is included in total revenue, not added on top. Alternative accelerators, memory-ramp constraints and China exposure temper the upside; the implied outcome crosses $400 billion but not $450 billion.\n40 | About $387 billion: Q1 revenue was $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance is $91 billion, an implied sequential increase of about 11.5%; carrying that pace forward for the remaining two quarters gives approximately $81.6B + $91B + $101.5B + $113.2B.\n     | The reference is Bloomberg’s $393 billion FY2027 sales projection, which sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion); the reported description of Wall Street consensus as “well over $300 billion” is too broad to place more precisely. Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion imply about 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that guided sequential pace through the remaining quarters gives an illustrative FY2027 total of about $387 billion. That is below Bloomberg’s estimate but remains within rung 3. Competition from custom chips and China-related demand risks, alongside reported memory constraints, temper the outlook but do not quantify a reduction that moves the forecast below $350 billion; customer and partnership announcements support demand but do not size additional FY2027 revenue. The CPU-sales dependency is part of total revenue and should not be added separately.\n41 | About $404 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1, roughly $94–95 billion for Q2, about $107–108 billion for Q3, and approximately $120 billion for Q4 assuming roughly 12% sequential growth from Q3.\n     | The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 3. The forecast is above that reference: Q1 was $81.6 billion; Q2 is estimated around $94–95 billion despite the $91 billion company guide, with upward estimate revisions and a prior-quarter beat supporting some upside to guidance; BofA sees potential Q3 guidance of $107–108 billion. Carrying roughly 12% sequential growth into Q4 gives about $120 billion and a total near $404 billion, above the $400 billion rung boundary. The $98.18 billion Q2 estimate is a less corroborated upside signal, while the $1 trillion order-book figure spans 2026 and 2027 and is not counted fully as FY2027 revenue. The approximately $20 billion FY2027 CPU-sales forecast in dependency v8 is a component of, not an addition to, this total.\n42 | About $403–404 billion: $81.6 billion Q1 actual, approximately $94–95 billion Q2, $107–108 billion Q3, and roughly $120 billion Q4 by carrying about 11.5% sequential growth from Q3.\n     | The $393 billion FY2027 consensus reference is rung 3, but it is not the forecast once the current-quarter evidence is incorporated. Q1 was $81.6 billion; using BofA’s $94–95 billion Q2 estimate, its $107–108 billion potential Q3 guide, and about 11.5% sequential growth into Q4 implies roughly $403–404 billion for FY2027—just over the $400 billion rung boundary. The estimate that repeating 12% sequential growth would leave revenue just under $400 billion is a meaningful countercase, so the crossing is narrow rather than secure. The roughly $20 billion Vera standalone-CPU forecast in v8 is part of total revenue, not an additional amount to add.\n43 | About $407 billion: add reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion to the Q3 guidance midpoint of $108 billion, then carry the roughly 12.3% Q2-to-Q3 sequential growth rate into Q4, implying about $121.3 billion.\n     | The implied FY2027 total is about $407 billion, which is above the $400 billion boundary for rung 2 but below the $450 billion boundary for rung 1. Supply constraints temper the extrapolation, but the current-quarter guidance is the strongest forecast input and carries the run-rate across $400 billion. The dependent stand-alone CPU forecast of about $20 billion is a component of this total and does not exceed it.\n44 | About $407 billion: $81.6 billion in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2 plus the $108 billion Q3 guide, with Q4 estimated at about $121 billion by carrying forward Q3’s roughly 12% sequential growth.\n     | The market reference is analysts’ $411 billion FY2027 expectation, which sits at rung 2. The reported Q1 and Q2 revenues plus the Q3 guide, carrying Q3’s approximately 12% sequential growth forward to Q4, imply about $407 billion for FY2027. That remains above rung 2’s $400 billion floor and below rung 1’s $450 billion threshold. Persistent supply and data-center bottlenecks temper the upside, while the announced pricing increase and substantial demand are not quantified as enough FY2027 revenue to cross a boundary. The approximately $20 billion stand-alone CPU outlook in v8 is part of total revenue, not an amount to add on top.\n45 | About $391 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1 + $91 billion guided for Q2 + the cited $103.1 billion Q3 estimate + about $115.5 billion for Q4 by carrying 12% sequential growth from Q3. The Q4 calculation and total are estimates, not reported results.\n     | The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, in rung 3. The evidence-based path is about $391 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion, the cited Q3 estimate of $103.1 billion, and Q4 of about $115.5 billion if Q3-to-Q4 growth is 12%. That leaves the forecast just below the $400 billion rung boundary. The roughly $1 trillion in Blackwell and Vera Rubin orders through 2027 and OpenAI purchases potentially worth $600 billion through 2030 support demand, but span products and periods and do not establish enough FY2027 revenue to lift this estimate across $400 billion. Supply tightness and China-related limits are counterweights. The $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU forecast in dependency v8 is included within, and does not exceed, the total-revenue forecast.\n46 | About $407 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1 plus $96.2 billion reported in Q2 plus the $108 billion Q3 midpoint guide, with Q4 estimated at about $121.3 billion by carrying forward the roughly 12.3% Q3 sequential growth rate.\n     | The reference is analysts’ $411 billion FY2027 expectation, which is rung 2. Independently, the reported Q1 and Q2 results plus the $108 billion Q3 guide imply about $407 billion using the Q3 sequential growth rate for Q4; that clears rung 2’s $400 billion floor but remains well below rung 1’s $450 billion threshold. Strong demand and planned pricing provide upside, but the additional evidence of persistent memory, packaging and data-center capacity constraints does not quantify enough FY2027 revenue to move the run-rate across a boundary. The $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin revenue target spans products and time periods and is not evidence that the volume is in hand for this fiscal year.\n47 | FY2027 revenue of about $407 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1 plus $96.2 billion reported in Q2, $108 billion guided for Q3, and an estimated roughly $121 billion for Q4 by carrying forward the approximately 12.3% sequential growth implied by Q2-to-Q3 guidance.\n     | The FY2027 market reference is LSEG's $396 billion estimate, which sits in rung 3. The reported Q1 and Q2 revenues plus Q3 guidance imply about $407 billion for the year: carrying Q3's roughly 12.3% sequential growth forward gives Q4 revenue of about $121 billion. That crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2, but remains below $450 billion. Memory, power and data-center capacity constraints and the longer-dated custom-chip threat temper the forecast; NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU-sales outlook is already part of total revenue, not incremental to it.\n48 | Approximately $403–404 billion: Q1 actual revenue of $81.6 billion, plus BofA’s $94–95 billion Q2 estimate and $107–108 billion potential Q3 guide, with about 11.5% sequential growth into Q4 (roughly $120 billion). The roughly $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU forecast in dependency v8 is part of total revenue, not an amount to add again.\n     | The reference is Bloomberg’s $393 billion FY2027 consensus, which sits in rung 3. The evidence implies roughly $403–404 billion: starting with Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion, using BofA’s $94–95 billion Q2 estimate and $107–108 billion potential Q3 guidance, then carrying about 11.5% sequential growth into Q4 gives approximately $120 billion for Q4. That is just above the $400 billion boundary; the company’s $91 billion Q2 guide and the estimate of just under $400 billion at 12% sequential growth are meaningful counterweights, but do not make the below-boundary outcome more likely.\n49 | About $390 billion for FY2027: the reported $81.6 billion Q1 and $91 billion Q2 guide, with Q2's 11% sequential growth carried forward through Q3 and Q4, imply about $386 billion before a modest demand-and-order uplift.\n     | The market reference is about $393 billion, which is rung 3. Carrying Q2's guided 11% sequential growth through Q3 and Q4 gives a base of about $386 billion ($81.6B + $91B + $101B + $112B); strong booked supply commitments and order visibility support a modest lift to roughly $390 billion. That remains below the $400 billion rung boundary. The $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin orders span products and periods, so they cannot be assigned in full to FY2027. Memory-supply and China-market risks temper the upside. NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU outlook is part of total revenue, not an addition to it.\n50 | Approximately $386 billion: take reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, then carry the roughly 11% sequential growth in the guidance forward to about $101 billion in Q3 and $112 billion in Q4. The resulting FY2027 total remains within rung 3.\n     | The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, which is rung 3; the 80%–82% growth estimates against FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion imply roughly $389–$393 billion, also rung 3. For the forecast, carrying Q2 guidance of $91 billion forward at roughly its 11% sequential growth rate gives about $101 billion in Q3 and $112 billion in Q4; with reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion, that implies approximately $386 billion for FY2027. The updated China-authorization theme, AWS deployment plans, and demand outlook provide upside, but the $1 trillion Blackwell/Vera Rubin forecast spans products and periods, and the $500 billion SK initiative is not an FY2027 revenue figure. The HBM ramp slowdown and China restrictions are counterweights. The dependency’s approximately $20 billion stand-alone CPU forecast is a component of total revenue and is consistent with this estimate, not additive to it. The implied outcome remains below the $400 billion boundary, so the forecast stays in rung 3.\n51 | Q1 actual revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion, the $103.1 billion Q3 estimate, and 11% sequential growth carried into Q4 imply about $390 billion. Demand and supplier-growth evidence justify a modest uplift to roughly $400–405 billion, with the approximately $1 trillion order figure discounted because it spans products and periods.\n     | The market reference is approximately $393 billion for FY2027, which is rung 3; the 80% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion is about $389 billion and also falls in rung 3. A mechanical path from reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion, Q2 guidance of $91 billion, the $103.1 billion Q3 estimate, and 11% sequential growth carried into Q4 gives roughly $390 billion. Strong AI-investment demand and upstream growth support a modest uplift from that baseline, while constrained HBM ramping, China exposure, and competing accelerators limit it. I therefore forecast about $400–405 billion: only a modest uplift is warranted from the roughly $1 trillion orders figure because it spans products and periods, and the forecast just crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2. The approximately $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU outlook is part of total revenue, not an amount to add on top.\n52 | About $418.5 billion, or roughly $419 billion: Q1 and Q2 reported revenue totals $177.8 billion; the Q3 estimate is about $113.5 billion; carrying the guided 12% sequential growth into Q4 implies about $127.1 billion.\n     | The $411 billion analyst expectation is in rung 2; Zacks’ 87.4% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion also implies roughly $404.5 billion, likewise rung 2. The forecast remains about $419 billion: FY2027 Q1 and Q2 total $177.8 billion; Q3 is estimated at about $113–114 billion, consistent with the 3.1% average beat above the high end of guidance over the past four quarters; carrying the guided 12% sequential growth forward gives Q4 of about $127 billion. This remains below the $450 billion rung-1 boundary. Supply constraints temper upside, and the roughly $15 billion stand-alone CPU forecast in v8 is included in—not added to—total revenue.\n53 | Approximately $400–410 billion, with a central estimate near $405 billion. Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion and an 11% sequential carry-forward through Q3 and Q4 implies about $386 billion; stronger order visibility and customer spending support a modest uplift, while the broad periods covered by the large product-order figures limit how much to assign to FY2027.\n     | The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion). The operating carry-forward from Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion, Q2 guidance of $91 billion, and roughly 11% sequential growth in each remaining quarter is about $386 billion. I infer approximately $400–410 billion (central estimate about $405 billion): the customer-spending and backlog evidence, strong order visibility and reported Blackwell/Rubin orders support a modest uplift to the carry-forward, while those order figures span products and periods and should not be attributed wholly to FY2027. That uplift is enough to make crossing $400 billion slightly more likely than not, but not enough to support $450 billion. The roughly $20 billion stand-alone CPU outlook is already part of total revenue, not an addition to it.\n54 | Carrying the sequential growth from Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion to Q2 guidance of $91 billion forward implies about $101.5 billion in Q3 and $113.2 billion in Q4, or roughly $387 billion for FY2027. Adding the dependency's approximately $19.5 billion stand-alone CPU forecast gives about $407 billion.\n     | Bloomberg's $393 billion projection is the clearest market reference and sits in rung 3; the separate “well over $300 billion” expectation is too broad to locate more precisely. The Q1 result and Q2 guidance imply a roughly $387 billion FY2027 run-rate if Q2's sequential growth carries through Q3 and Q4, below the $400 billion boundary. Adding the dependency's roughly $19.5 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU forecast, which is distinct from the existing revenue run-rate, gives about $407 billion and crosses into rung 2. This remains a forecast rather than an established result: the AWS deployment plan and $119 billion in supply-related commitments are not specified as FY2027 revenue, while HBM ramp constraints and custom-chip alternatives are downside risks.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import pathlib,random,sqlite3,sys\nc=sqlite3.connect((pathlib.Path(sys.argv[1])/'\\\\''linked-1-copy/views.sqlite'\\\\'').as_uri()+'\\\\''?mode=ro'\\\\'',uri=True)\nr=c.execute(\\\"select judgement_id,publication_day,implied_outcome,reasons from judgements where view_id='\\\\''v14'\\\\'' and publication_day>='\\\\''2026-07-10'\\\\'' order by judgement_id\\\").fetchall()\nrandom.Random(37).shuffle(r)\nfor i,(jid,day,forecast,reasons) in enumerate(r,1):\n if int(sys.argv[2])<=i<=int(sys.argv[3]):print(f\\\"{i:02d} | {forecast}\\\\n     | {reasons}\\\")\n if len(sys.argv)>4:print(i,jid,day)\n' '/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad' 55 71\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import pathlib,random,sqlite3,sys\nc=sqlite3.connect((pathlib.Path(sys.argv[1])/'\\\\''linked-1-copy/views.sqlite'\\\\'').as_uri()+'\\\\''?mode=ro'\\\\'',uri=True)\nr=c.execute(\\\"select judgement_id,publication_day,implied_outcome,reasons from judgements where view_id='\\\\''v14'\\\\'' and publication_day>='\\\\''2026-07-10'\\\\'' order by judgement_id\\\").fetchall()\nrandom.Random(37).shuffle(r)\nfor i,(jid,day,forecast,reasons) in enumerate(r,1):\n if int(sys.argv[2])<=i<=int(sys.argv[3]):print(f\\\"{i:02d} | {forecast}\\\\n     | {reasons}\\\")\n if len(sys.argv)>4:print(i,jid,day)\n' '/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad' 55 71\"","aggregated_output":"55 | Approximately $387 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion followed by Q2 guidance of $91 billion implies about 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that guided pace forward gives roughly $101.5 billion in Q3 and $113.2 billion in Q4, for about $387 billion total.\n     | The market reference is Bloomberg’s $393 billion FY2027 projection, which sits in rung 3; the separate description of Wall Street expectations as “well over $300 billion” is too broad to place more precisely. The guided run-rate implies about $387 billion: starting with Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, carrying the implied 11.5% sequential growth through Q3 and Q4 gives approximately $101.5 billion and $113.2 billion, respectively. Added hyperscaler-spending support and NVIDIA demand announcements are offset by memory constraints, custom-chip competition, and China-related risks, but none quantifies a change large enough to move the estimate across the $350 billion or $400 billion boundary. The dependency forecast of $18–19 billion in stand-alone CPU sales is a component of total revenue, not an additional amount to add to this total estimate.\n56 | About $407.1 billion: $81.6 billion Q1 plus $96.2 billion Q2, plus $108 billion Q3 guidance, plus approximately $121.3 billion for Q4 by carrying Q3's 12.3% sequential growth forward.\n     | The LSEG FY2027 consensus is $396 billion, which sits in rung 3. The reported first two quarters total $177.8 billion; adding Q3 guidance of $108 billion and carrying its 12.3% sequential growth forward for Q4 implies about $121.3 billion, or roughly $407.1 billion for FY2027. That is above the $400 billion boundary but below $450 billion, so the forecast remains in rung 2. Supply and memory constraints temper the outlook but do not quantify a reduction sufficient to pull the implied total below $400 billion. The v8 stand-alone CPU forecast is part of total revenue and is consistent with this estimate, not additive to it.\n57 | About $407.1 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1 plus $96.2 billion reported in Q2, $108.0 billion guided for Q3, and approximately $121.3 billion for Q4 by carrying forward the 12.3% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance.\n     | The market reference is LSEG’s $396 billion FY2027 consensus, which sits in rung 3. The evidence-based run-rate estimate is about $407.1 billion: reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus reported Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion, Q3 guidance of $108 billion, and Q4 of about $121.3 billion if Q3’s 12.3% sequential growth carries forward. That clears the $400 billion rung boundary but remains below $450 billion, so the forecast is rung 2. The reported demand-versus-supply constraint and the memory-shortage outlook temper confidence, but do not quantify a FY2027 reduction that would displace this estimate. The stand-alone CPU dependency’s approximately $20 billion forecast is consistent with the total and does not exceed it.\n58 | Using Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.615 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, the 11.5% sequential increase carried forward gives approximately $101.5 billion for Q3 and $113.2 billion for Q4, or about $387 billion for FY2027. The approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU estimate is included within, not added to, total company revenue.\n     | The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 projection, which is in rung 3. Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion followed by Q2 guidance of $91 billion implies 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that rate through Q3 and Q4 implies about $387 billion for FY2027, still in rung 3 and below the $400 billion boundary. The approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU forecast is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add to this company-wide run-rate. Strong orders and the UAE export easing support the outlook, while the stated China exclusion and HBM supply concerns weigh against it; none provides a quantified FY2027 adjustment that moves the run-rate across a rung boundary.\n59 | About $415 billion. Carrying the roughly 11% sequential increase in NVIDIA's $91 billion Q2 guide forward gives an operating base near $386 billion for FY2027; customer investment, demand visibility, and some improved supply support a modest uplift, while China exposure and memory constraints temper it.\n     | The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion). The Q1 actual and Q2 guide imply a roughly $386 billion base if the guided 11% sequential growth is carried forward; strong customer investment and demand, plus improved supply support, justify a modest uplift to about $415 billion. China-related limits, memory constraints, and the fact that announced GPU deployments and orders span products and periods keep the forecast below $450 billion. The resulting estimate crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2. NVIDIA’s approximately $20 billion stand-alone CPU-sales forecast is a component of total revenue, not an additional amount to add to the total estimate; the total remains above that dependency’s conclusion.\n60 | About $410 billion (roughly $405–415 billion): Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion, carrying the guided sequential growth through the remaining quarters, implies a base near $386 billion; customer demand and spending evidence supports a modest uplift.\n     | The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion). Carrying forward the guided growth from Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion gives a roughly $386 billion operating base; strong customer spending and orders support a modest uplift, while supply constraints, China exposure, and spending that bypasses NVIDIA limit it. The implied estimate of about $410 billion crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2, but does not support $450 billion. The $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin orders span products and periods, and planned GPU deployments are not all FY2027 revenue.\n61 | About $419 billion: Q1 and Q2 total $177.8 billion; Q3 is estimated at about $113.6 billion by applying the 3.1% average beat to the high end of guidance, and Q4 at about $127.2 billion by carrying forward the guided 12% sequential growth.\n     | The market reference is the $411 billion FY2027 analyst estimate, which sits in rung 2; Zacks’ 87.4% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion also implies roughly $405 billion, in rung 2. The evidence-based estimate is about $419 billion: Q1 and Q2 revenue total $177.8 billion; applying the reported 3.1% average beat to the high end of Q3 guidance gives about $113.6 billion for Q3, and carrying the guided 12% sequential growth into Q4 gives about $127.2 billion. Supply constraints through FY2028 temper the upside, while the specific Q3 estimate and strong demand signals support the run-rate; neither shifts the estimate across $400 billion or $450 billion. The roughly $15 billion stand-alone CPU estimate in v8 is included within total revenue, not added to it.\n62 | Q1 actual revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion, with 11% sequential growth carried through Q3 and Q4, implies about $386 billion. Strong cloud demand and spending support a modest uplift to roughly $393 billion, partly offset by supply constraints and China exposure.\n     | The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, which is rung 3. Independently, Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, carrying the 11% sequential growth through the remaining quarters, imply about $386 billion ($81.6B + $91B + $101B + $112B). Strong cloud growth and spending support a modest uplift from that run-rate to roughly $393 billion; memory-supply constraints and China exposure limit the uplift. This remains below the $400 billion boundary. The roughly $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin figure spans products and periods, so it is not counted as FY2027 revenue. NVIDIA’s roughly $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU outlook is included within total revenue, not added to it.\n63 | About $407 billion: $81.6B reported in Q1 plus $96.2B in Q2 and the $108B Q3 midpoint guide, then about $121B for Q4 by carrying forward Q3's roughly 12.3% sequential growth.\n     | The market reference is approximately $411 billion, which sits in rung 2. The run-rate implied by the reported first two quarters and Q3 guidance is about $407 billion: $81.6B + $96.2B + $108B, with Q4 estimated at about $121B by carrying forward Q3's roughly 12.3% sequential growth. That is above the $400B boundary but below $450B. Supply and memory constraints temper the outlook, but the evidence does not make a Q4 slowdown below roughly $114B—the level needed to fall under $400B—the most likely outcome. The roughly $20B standalone-CPU forecast in v8 is part of total revenue, not an addition to it.\n64 | About $391 billion on the operating path: $81.6 billion reported in Q1, $91 billion guided for Q2, $103.1 billion estimated for Q3, and approximately $115.5 billion for Q4 if sequential growth continues at roughly 12%. The demand commitments support the upper end, but their multi-year periods do not establish enough FY2027 shipments to lift this estimate to $400 billion.\n     | The market reference is approximately $393 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 3. The forecast remains near the top of that rung, not because consensus alone keeps it there: Q1 revenue was $81.6 billion, Q2 guidance is $91 billion, and the cited Q3 estimate is $103.1 billion. Carrying roughly 12% sequential growth into Q4 implies about $115.5 billion, or roughly $391 billion for the year; the separate estimate of just under $400 billion on that growth path is consistent with the same rung. Strong demand, customer commitments and higher hyperscaler capex support the upper end, but the large commitments span products and years and do not establish enough FY2027 shipments to imply $400 billion or more. Memory and China-related constraints are counterweights. The roughly $20 billion standalone-CPU forecast in v8 is a component of total revenue and is consistent with, not additional to, this total.\n65 | Q1 and Q2 actual revenue total $177.8 billion; adding Q3 midpoint guidance of $108 billion gives $285.8 billion through Q3. Carrying the roughly 12.3% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance forward gives Q4 of about $121.3 billion and FY2027 total revenue of approximately $407.1 billion.\n     | The implied outcome is above the $400 billion rung boundary but below $450 billion, so the forecast remains rung 2. The $411 billion market expectation is a reference, not the reason to stay there. Q2 actual revenue and Q3 guidance support a roughly $407 billion run-rate; the report that NVIDIA raised guidance for the rest of the year tilts upward but gives no amount to add. Demand and pricing signals are offset by supply constraints, and the large order figure is not allocated to FY2027 revenue. The roughly $20 billion stand-alone CPU forecast in v8 is part of total revenue, not an addition to it.\n66 | Approximately $385.7 billion: start with reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.615 billion and guided Q2 revenue of $91.0 billion, then carry the stated 11% sequential growth rate forward to Q3 ($101.0 billion) and Q4 ($112.1 billion).\n     | The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 3; the 82% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $393 billion as well. The stronger near-term forecast input is NVIDIA's Q2 FY2027 guidance of $91 billion, described as up 11% sequentially. Carrying that guided sequential growth forward gives approximately $81.615B + $91.0B + $101.0B + $112.1B = $385.7B for FY2027. Strong demand supports the run-rate, but the customer commitments span multiple periods and do not establish FY2027 revenue sufficient to cross $400 billion; memory constraints and China-related limits weigh against that move. The v8 CPU forecast of about $17 billion is part of total revenue, not an amount to add to it.\n67 | Q1 and Q2 reported revenue total $177.8 billion; adding Q3 guidance of $108 billion and carrying the 12.3% sequential growth rate into Q4 implies about $121.3 billion for Q4 and approximately $407.1 billion for FY2027.\n     | The current market reference is the approximately $411 billion Wall Street expectation, which is rung 2; the earlier LSEG estimate of $396 billion was rung 3. NVIDIA’s reported Q1 and Q2 revenue total $177.8 billion, and adding Q3 midpoint guidance of $108 billion plus Q4 at the guided 12.3% sequential growth rate implies about $407.1 billion for FY2027. That is below the latest analyst reference but still above the $400 billion rung boundary. Supply constraints and memory availability temper the forecast, while the unquantified report that guidance was raised does not justify a larger numerical adjustment. The roughly $20 billion stand-alone CPU outlook in v8 is part of total revenue, not additive, and is consistent with this total.\n68 | Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion and Q3 guidance of $108 billion total $285.8 billion. Carrying the roughly 12.3% sequential growth from Q2 to Q3 forward implies Q4 revenue of about $121.3 billion and FY2027 total revenue of about $407 billion.\n     | The market reference is LSEG's $396 billion FY2027 estimate, which is rung 3. The reported Q1 and Q2 totals plus Q3 guidance imply about $407 billion: carrying the roughly 12.3% sequential growth from Q2 to Q3 forward gives Q4 revenue of about $121 billion. That clears the $400 billion boundary but remains below $450 billion. The stated supply constraints temper the upside, while the dependent stand-alone CPU forecast of about $20 billion is a component of, and does not exceed, the implied total.\n69 | About $387 billion: $81.6 billion in Q1 and $91 billion guided for Q2 imply roughly 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that growth forward gives approximately $101.5 billion in Q3 and $113.2 billion in Q4, totaling about $387 billion.\n     | The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 projection, which sits in rung 3; the separate description of Wall Street expectations as “well over $300 billion” is too broad to place more precisely. Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion followed by Q2 guidance of $91 billion implies about 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that guided growth forward gives roughly $101.5 billion in Q3 and $113.2 billion in Q4, or about $387 billion for FY2027. That is below the $400 billion boundary and remains in rung 3. The disclosed AWS deployment plan and hyperscaler spending outlook support demand, while the HBM ramp slowdown and tighter China-related controls weigh the other way; neither supplies a quantified FY2027 revenue adjustment sufficient to cross a rung boundary. NVIDIA's $18–19 billion stand-alone CPU sales forecast is included within total revenue, not added on top, and is consistent with the whole-revenue estimate.\n70 | About $418 billion: add reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion to a Q3 estimate of roughly $113.5 billion (the midpoint of the $113–$114 billion forecast, supported by the reported guidance-beat history), then carry the 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance into Q4, giving about $127 billion.\n     | The market reference is Wall Street analysts’ $411 billion FY2027 estimate, which is in rung 2; Zacks’ 87.4% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion implies about $405 billion, also rung 2. The evidence-based estimate is higher, around $418 billion, but remains below rung 1’s $450 billion boundary. It incorporates the $113–$114 billion Q3 analyst forecast and the recent history of beats over guidance, then carries the 12% sequential growth implied by management’s Q3 guide into Q4. Persistent supply and downstream data-center constraints temper the upside. NVIDIA’s approximately $20 billion stand-alone CPU outlook is part of total revenue, not an amount to add on top; v8’s conclusion is consistent with that treatment.\n71 | Approximately $387 billion for FY2027: Q1 revenue was $81.615 billion and Q2 guidance is $91 billion, an 11.5% sequential increase; extending that sequential growth through Q3 and Q4 gives about $101.5 billion and $113.2 billion, respectively, for roughly $387.3 billion total.\n     | The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which is in rung 3; Zacks' 80% growth estimate, applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, also implies about $389 billion in rung 3. The reported Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion imply 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that rate into Q3 and Q4 gives approximately $387 billion for FY2027. That is about $13 billion below rung 2's $400 billion boundary, while remaining above rung 3's $350 billion floor. Strong orders, AWS's planned GPU deployment, and Wistron's mass production support the outlook, but the disclosed deployment spans two fiscal years and production plans spanning several years are not FY2027 revenue in hand. China exclusions and memory constraints weigh against a higher forecast. The dependency forecast of roughly $17 billion in stand-alone CPU sales is a component of total revenue and is consistent with, not additive to, the approximately $387 billion total.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import collections,json,pathlib,re,random,sqlite3,sys\nsys.path[:0]=['\\\\''src'\\\\'','\\\\''docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window'\\\\'']\nfrom hivemind.views import judging\nimport contradictions,verdict\nb=pathlib.Path(sys.argv[1]); lc=b/'\\\\''linked-1-copy'\\\\''; cc=b/'\\\\''control-1-copy'\\\\''\ndef op(p):\n c=sqlite3.connect(p.as_uri()+'\\\\''?mode=ro'\\\\'',uri=True); c.row_factory=sqlite3.Row; return c\nl,c=op(lc/'\\\\''views.sqlite'\\\\''),op(cc/'\\\\''views.sqlite'\\\\'')\ns={name:json.loads((folder/'\\\\''summary.json'\\\\'').read_text()) for name,folder in [('\\\\''linked-1'\\\\'',lc),('\\\\''control-1'\\\\'',cc)]}\nL,C=s['\\\\''linked-1'\\\\''],s['\\\\''control-1'\\\\'']\ndays=lambda s:{(j['\\\\''view'\\\\''],j['\\\\''day'\\\\'']) for j in s['\\\\''reader judgements'\\\\'']}\nm=days(L)&days(C)\nprint('\\\\''matched'\\\\'',len(m),'\\\\''by view'\\\\'',collections.Counter(v for v,d in m),'\\\\''v21 Sept8 matched link/ctrl'\\\\'',[('\\\\''v21'\\\\'','\\\\''2026-09-08'\\\\'') in days(x) for x in [L,C]])\nfor n,summary in s.items():\n r=[(d['\\\\''call_id'\\\\''],d['\\\\''view'\\\\''],d['\\\\''day'\\\\'']) for d in summary['\\\\''archive judge rows written (dated)'\\\\''] if d['\\\\''refused'\\\\''] and d['\\\\''reader'\\\\'']]\n print(n,'\\\\''matched'\\\\'', [x for x in r if x[1:] in m],'\\\\''unmatched'\\\\'',[x for x in r if x[1:] not in m])\n print(n,'\\\\''all new rows'\\\\'',len(summary['\\\\''archive judge rows written (dated)'\\\\'']),'\\\\''transport attempts'\\\\'',len(summary['\\\\''judge attempts at the transport'\\\\'']), '\\\\''nonreader refusals'\\\\'',[x for x in summary['\\\\''archive judge rows written (dated)'\\\\''] if x['\\\\''refused'\\\\''] and not x['\\\\''reader'\\\\'']])\n g=[j for j in summary['\\\\''archive judge rows written (dated)'\\\\''] if j['\\\\''reader'\\\\''] and (j['\\\\''view'\\\\''],j['\\\\''day'\\\\'']) in m]\n print(n,'\\\\''matched fresh archive attempts'\\\\'',len(g))\nprint('\\\\''verdict H1'\\\\'',verdict.h1({f'\\\\''{label}-{i}'\\\\'':s[label+'\\\\''-1'\\\\''] for i in (1,2,3) for label in ('\\\\''linked'\\\\'','\\\\''control'\\\\'')}))\nar=op(lc/'\\\\''archive.sqlite'\\\\'')\nprepared=[]\nfor r in ar.execute(\\\"select call_id,request_json,request_sha256,answer_json,response_json from model_calls where purpose='\\\\''judge'\\\\'' and call_id>8737 order by call_id\\\"):\n t=json.loads(r['\\\\''request_json'\\\\''])['\\\\''messages'\\\\''][-1]['\\\\''content'\\\\'']\n data=json.loads(r['\\\\''answer_json'\\\\''])\n split=t.partition('\\\\''\\\\n\\\\nThe code returned the previous answer because: '\\\\'')\n prepared.append((r,t,data,split[0],split[2]))\nfor r,t,d,orig,err in prepared:\n if '\\\\'':refused:'\\\\'' not in r['\\\\''request_sha256'\\\\'']: continue\n ident=r['\\\\''call_id'\\\\'']; cid=lambda x:x[0]['\\\\''call_id'\\\\'']\n deps=set(re.findall(r'\\\\''\"'^- (v'\"\\\\d+) \\\\('\\\\'',orig.partition('\\\\''[Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]'\\\\'')[2].split('\\\\''\\\\n\\\\n['\\\\'',1)[0],re.M))\n evidence=judging.evidence_shown_in(t);themes=judging.themes_shown_in(t)\n badpush={p['\\\\''evidence_id'\\\\''] for p in d['\\\\''pushes'\\\\'']}-evidence\n badcite=set(d['\\\\''cites'\\\\''])-evidence\n badview=set(d['\\\\''cites_views'\\\\''])-deps\n badtheme={p['\\\\''view_id'\\\\''] for p in d['\\\\''theme_pushes'\\\\'']}-themes\n follow=[(cid(x),x[4].split('\\\\''\\\\n'\\\\'')[0],'\\\\'':refused:'\\\\'' in x[0]['\\\\''request_sha256'\\\\'']) for x in prepared if x[3]==orig and cid(x)>ident and x[4]]\n print(ident,t.splitlines()[0].split('\\\\'' ('\\\\'')[0],'\\\\''evidence count'\\\\'',len(evidence),'\\\\''bad cites'\\\\'',badcite,'\\\\''bad push'\\\\'',badpush,'\\\\''bad cites_views'\\\\'',badview,'\\\\''bad theme_pushes'\\\\'',badtheme,\n '\\\\''raw same'\\\\'',json.loads(json.loads(r['\\\\''response_json'\\\\''])['\\\\''choices'\\\\''][0]['\\\\''message'\\\\'']['\\\\''content'\\\\''])==d,'\\\\''deps'\\\\'',deps,'\\\\''theme-like invalids'\\\\'',badview&themes,'\\\\''retry'\\\\'',follow)\n if ident==8777:print('\\\\''e693 one edit candidates'\\\\'',[i for i in sorted(evidence) if (len(i)==len('\\\\''e693'\\\\'')+1 and any(i[:j]+i[j+1:]=='\\\\''e693'\\\\'' for j in range(len(i)))) or (len(i)==len('\\\\''e693'\\\\'') and sum(x\"'!=y for x,y in zip(i,'\"'\\\\''e693'\\\\''))==1)])\nrr=l.execute(\\\"select judgement_id,publication_day,call_id,implied_outcome,reasons from judgements where view_id='\\\\''v14'\\\\'' and publication_day>='\\\\''2026-07-10'\\\\'' order by judgement_id\\\").fetchall()\nr=list(rr);random.Random(37).shuffle(r)\nprint('\\\\''independent mask double counts'\\\\'',[(i,tuple(r[i-1])[:3]) for i in (7,20,28,54)])\nprint('\\\\''independent mask no CPU mentions'\\\\'',[(i,tuple(r[i-1])[:3]) for i in (32,46,60)])\nprint('\\\\''mentions of CPU or v8 by field'\\\\'',collections.Counter((bool(re.search(r'\\\\''\\\\bcpu\\\\b|\\\\bv8\\\\b'\\\\'',x[3],re.I)),bool(re.search(r'\\\\''\\\\bcpu\\\\b|\\\\bv8\\\\b'\\\\'',x[4],re.I))) for x in rr))\nprint('\\\\''mentions in forecast'\\\\'',[(x[0],x[1]) for x in rr if re.search(r'\\\\''\\\\bcpu\\\\b|\\\\bv8\\\\b'\\\\'',x[3],re.I)])\nprint('\\\\''period profiles'\\\\'',[(x[0],tuple(x)[1:]) for x in l.execute(\\\"select v.view_id,v.object_id,v.question,k.measure,k.scope,k.period_start,k.period_end,k.unit from views v join view_keys k using(view_id) where v.view_id in ('\\\\''v8'\\\\'','\\\\''v9'\\\\'','\\\\''v14'\\\\'','\\\\''v16'\\\\'','\\\\''v21'\\\\'') order by v.view_id\\\")])\nfor name,conn in [('\\\\''linked'\\\\'',l),('\\\\''control'\\\\'',c)]:\n rows=contradictions.daily(conn,'\\\\''v9'\\\\'','\\\\''v14'\\\\'','\\\\''2026-07-10'\\\\'','\\\\''2026-09-07'\\\\'')\n print(name,'\\\\''shared summary'\\\\'',contradictions.summary(rows))\n if name=='\\\\''linked'\\\\'':\n  whole=contradictions.daily(conn,'\\\\''v9'\\\\'','\\\\''v14'\\\\'','\\\\''2026-07-10'\\\\'','\\\\''2026-09-20'\\\\'')\n  print('\\\\''whole'\\\\'',contradictions.summary(whole))\n  for row in whole:\n   if row[1] in ('\\\\''definite'\\\\'','\\\\''overlap'\\\\''): print('\\\\''link flagged'\\\\'',row)\n else:\n  for row in rows:\n   if row[0][5:] in ('\\\\''07-15'\\\\'','\\\\''08-19'\\\\'','\\\\''08-23'\\\\'','\\\\''08-25'\\\\'','\\\\''09-06'\\\\''): print('\\\\''chosen control'\\\\'',row)\n' '/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
