', '2026-02-01', '2027-01-31', 'percent') v9 ('NVDA', 'segment_revenue', 'Data Center', '2026-02-01', '2027-01-31', 'USD')\nv9 ('NVDA', 'segment_revenue', 'Data Center', '2026-02-01', '2027-01-31', 'USD') v14 ('NVDA', 'total_revenue', '', '2026-02-01', '2027-01-31', 'USD')\nDRAW 3 [['v14', 'v8', 'first_is_bullish', 148], ['v16', 'v14', 'first_is_bullish', 265], ['v16', 'v8', 'first_is_bullish', 265], ['v21', 'v16', 'first_is_bullish', 534], ['v21', 'v9', 'first_is_bullish', 534], ['v9', 'v14', 'first_is_bullish', 148]]\nv14 ('NVDA', 'total_revenue', '', '2026-02-01', '2027-01-31', 'USD') v8 ('NVDA', 'other', 'NVIDIA stand-alone CPU', '2026-02-01', '2027-01-31', 'USD')\nv16 ('NVDA', 'product_revenue', 'total CPU', '2026-02-01', '2027-01-31', 'USD') v14 ('NVDA', 'total_revenue', '', '2026-02-01', '2027-01-31', 'USD')\nv16 ('NVDA', 'product_revenue', 'total CPU', '2026-02-01', '2027-01-31', 'USD') v8 ('NVDA', 'other', 'NVIDIA stand-alone CPU', '2026-02-01', '2027-01-31', 'USD')\nv21 ('NVDA', 'growth_rate', 'total revenue', '2026-02-01', '2027-01-31', 'percent') v16 ('NVDA', 'product_revenue', 'total CPU', '2026-02-01', '2027-01-31', 'USD')\nv21 ('NVDA', 'growth_rate', 'total revenue', '2026-02-01', '2027-01-31', 'percent') v9 ('NVDA', 'segment_revenue', 'Data Center', '2026-02-01', '2027-01-31', 'USD')\nv9 ('NVDA', 'segment_revenue', 'Data Center', '2026-02-01', '2027-01-31', 'USD') v14 ('NVDA', 'total_revenue', '', '2026-02-01', '2027-01-31', 'USD')\nplanned Sep19 v21 (1,)\nAMBIG 2026-08-05 About $354 billion: the total-revenue dependency forecasts roughly $393 billion for FY2027, and applying the approximately 90% Data Center share estimate gives $393 billion × 90% ≈ $354 billion. Q1’s 92% actual share is supportive, but the 90% forward estimate is the basis for the FY2027 projection. Q1 actual revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion, with 11% sequential growth carried through Q3 and Q4, implies about $386 billion. Strong cloud demand and spending support a modest uplift to roughly $393 billion, partly offset by supply constraints and China exposure.\nAMBIG 2026-08-06 Roughly $375–385 billion for FY2027 Data Center revenue. This starts from the prior roughly $354 billion estimate, incorporates SemiAnalysis’s 20% second-half upside view directionally, and discounts the unquantified uplift so the segment remains below the $390–395 billion total-revenue forecast and leaves room for other segments. Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion, with the guided 11% sequential growth carried through Q3 and Q4, implies about $386 billion. Customer spending and order/supply signals support a modest uplift, while memory and China headwinds temper it, giving an implied FY2027 total of roughly $390–395 billion.\nAMBIG 2026-08-08 The current whole-company forecast of about $405–415 billion, multiplied by a 90–92% Data Center share, implies roughly $365–382 billion before an uplift. Q1 growth, the SemiAnalysis second-half estimate, and customer-demand evidence support a central estimate around $390 billion, still below $400 billion. About $410 billion (roughly $405–415 billion): Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion, carrying the guided sequential growth through the remaining quarters, implies a base near $386 billion; customer demand and spending evidence supports a modest uplift.\nAMBIG 2026-08-13 About $368–377 billion of FY2027 Data Center revenue: the current v14 total-revenue forecast of $400–410 billion multiplied by the recent reported Data Center share of about 92%. This is a forecast allocation of the whole-company outlook, not an annualization of Q1 alone. The Q1 actual, Q2 guidance, Q3 estimate and continued sequential growth into Q4 imply a baseline around $390 billion. Allowing a modest uplift for demand and booked orders, while discounting multi-period orders and competitive, supply and China risks, implies roughly $400–410 billion.\nAMBIG 2026-08-15 The current FY2027 total-revenue forecast in v14 is about $400–405 billion; applying the reported 92% Data Center mix implies approximately $368–373 billion. That remains below $400 billion and within rung 3. Q1 actual revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion, the $103.1 billion Q3 estimate, and 11% sequential growth carried into Q4 imply about $390 billion. Demand and supplier-growth evidence justify a modest uplift to roughly $400–405 billion, with the approximately $1 trillion order figure discounted because it spans products and periods.\nAMBIG 2026-08-20 The v14 total-revenue forecast of approximately $403.5 billion, applied at the observed 92% Data Center mix, implies about $371 billion for the segment. The quantified 20% H2 outperformance view is an upside risk, but its H2 base is unstated and applying it mechanically would imply about $413 billion—more than the supplied whole-company forecast. Approximately $403.5 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1, about $94.5 billion for Q2 based on the $94–95 billion estimate, about $107.5 billion for Q3 per BofA, and about $119.9 billion for Q4 by carrying roughly 11.5% sequential growth forward. The $20 billion FY2027 CPU forecast in v8 is part of this total, not additional revenue.\nAMBIG 2026-08-28 Q1 and Q2 Data Center revenue totaled about $164.3 billion ($75.25 billion plus $89 billion). Applying the observed Q2 Data Center share of 92.5% to Q3 revenue guidance of $108 billion implies about $99.9 billion for Q3; carrying that roughly 12.2% sequential growth forward implies about $112.1 billion for Q4, or approximately $376 billion for FY2027. Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion and Q3 guidance of $108 billion total $285.8 billion. Carrying the roughly 12.3% sequential growth from Q2 to Q3 forward implies Q4 revenue of about $121.3 billion and FY2027 total revenue of about $407 billion.\nAMBIG 2026-09-08 Approximately $376 billion for FY2027: Q1 and Q2 Data Center revenue total $164.25 billion; estimate Q3 at about $99.9 billion by applying the latest 92.5% mix to the $108 billion total-revenue guide; then estimate Q4 at about $112.2 billion by carrying forward the roughly 12.3% sequential total-revenue growth implied by the guide and applying the same mix. This is below $400 billion and consistent with v14's approximately $407.1 billion whole-company forecast. Q1 and Q2 actual revenue total $177.8 billion; adding Q3 midpoint guidance of $108 billion gives $285.8 billion through Q3. Carrying the roughly 12.3% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance forward gives Q4 of about $121.3 billion and FY2027 total revenue of approximately $407.1 billion.\nlinked Counter({('v14', False): 71, ('v9', False): 71, ('v16', False): 65, ('v21', False): 54, ('v16', True): 3, ('v14', True): 2, ('v9', True): 1, ('v21', True): 1})\ncontrol Counter({('v14', False): 59, ('v9', False): 58, ('v16', False): 44, ('v21', False): 38, ('v14', True): 1, ('v21', True): 1})\nNo exact reference to a dependency in accepted DC forecast, review list:\n436 2026-08-06 Roughly $375–385 billion for FY2027 Data Center revenue. This starts from the prior roughly $354 billion estimate, incorporates SemiAnalysis’s 20% second-half upside view directionally, and discounts the unquantified uplift so the segment remains below the $390–395 billion total-revenue forecast and leaves room for other segments. not be carried through in full without conflicting with the total-revenue constraint and leaving no room for other segments. The new SpaceX capacity disclosures support demand but do not state NVIDIA revenue or FY2027 shipment timing. On balance, roughly $375–385 billion is implied: above $350 billion, but below $400 billion, so the rung remains 3.\n835 2026-08-25 About $370–375 billion for FY2027: use reported Q1 Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion, the roughly $85–86 billion Q2 estimates, then carry approximately 14% sequential growth forward through Q3 and Q4. This remains below the dependent FY2027 total-revenue forecast of about $404–405 billion. he reference but remains within rung 3’s $350–400 billion range. It is also below the dependent FY2027 total-revenue forecast of about $404–405 billion. Competition, export-policy risks and memory constraints temper the upside; the $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin figure spans products and periods and is not a direct FY2027 Data Center revenue estimate.\nreader places in v9 71\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
